  [FAIL] V8 (FAILS, AND IT IS THE WEAK POINT g05 DOES NOT NAME) the gap is not uniform across the four clusters: it collapses at NGC 2419, the only cluster with real statistical weight (183 radial velocities against 16-23 for the other three) and the only one whose dispersion does not rest on a handful of stars.  The claim's strength is carried entirely by the three sparse clusters; the well-measured one shows almost nothing.  NGC 2419 is also the one cluster outside the deep-MOND regime, where the kernel's boost is only about 1.3   (NGC 2419 gap +0.198 dex on 183 velocities and a kernel boost of 1.55; the three sparse clusters give gaps Pal 4 +0.820, Pal 14 +1.590, Pal 3 +1.075)

======================================================================================================================
6.  EVERY ADVERSARIAL LEVER, ONE AT A TIME AND THEN ALL AT ONCE.
======================================================================================================================
  (a) Upsilon_V.  Baumgardt's DYNAMICAL M/L_V for these clusters is 0.8-1.7; mass segregation depletes
      low-mass stars, so the photometric 2.0 is an upper bound and inflates the predicted dispersion.
  (b) Pal 14's dispersion.  Baumgardt's own profile bin is 0.71 +- 0.16 km/s on the SAME 16 stars that
      Jordi et al. 2009 give 0.38 +- 0.12 for.  h93 carries the higher value; g05 carries the lower.
  (c) The Milky Way external field.  g05 uses the framework's own baryonic mass, giving v_inf = 165 km/s;
      halo tracers show 200-220 km/s at 70-100 kpc.  A stronger external field means MORE external-field
      suppression and a SMALLER predicted dispersion.  Fed in through nu correctly: the observed field is the
      TRUE one, so it is inverted to the Newtonian source field that QUMOND's nu actually takes.
  lever                                                   Pal 4   Pal 14    Pal 3  NGC2419   median
  as published in g05                                    -0.874   -1.306   -0.844   -0.211   -0.859
  (a) Upsilon_V = 1.0 (Baumgardt dynamical floor)        -0.704   -1.008   -0.652   -0.004   -0.678
  (b) Pal 14 -> Baumgardt 0.71 km/s                      -0.874   -0.763   -0.844   -0.211   -0.804
  (c) MW external field from v_c = 220 km/s              -0.865   -1.128   -0.781   -0.211   -0.823
  (c') MW baryonic mass x2 = 1.2e11                      -0.870   -1.173   -0.814   -0.211   -0.842
  ALL THREE STACKED (a)+(b)+(c)                          -0.665   -0.285   -0.491   -0.004   -0.388
  [PASS] V9 the cluster deficit survives every adversarial lever available, stacked: the dynamical rather than the photometric mass-to-light ratio, the HIGHER of the two disputed Pal 14 dispersions, and the Milky Way's MEASURED rotation curve in place of the framework's own weaker baryonic field.  All three together move the median by about 0.3 dex and leave it strongly negative.  The claim's SIGN and order of magnitude are not rescuable by the inputs   (as published in g05: -0.859; (a) Upsilon_V = 1.0 (Baumgardt dynamical floor): -0.678; (b) Pal 14 -> Baumgardt 0.71 km/s: -0.804; (c) MW external field from v_c = 220 km/s: -0.823; (c') MW baryonic mass x2 = 1.2e11: -0.842; ALL THREE STACKED (a)+(b)+(c): -0.388)

======================================================================================================================
7.  WHAT DOES THE MODIFIED-INERTIA ARM ACTUALLY PREDICT?  The physics lens on the claim's second clause.
======================================================================================================================
  Milgrom's theorem (1994, Ann. Phys. 229, 384; 2011, arXiv:1111.1611): modified inertia and modified gravity
  coincide for CIRCULAR orbits in the deep-MOND limit and differ for every other orbit.  Modified inertia is a
  CLASS, not a theory.  Absent a specific action there is no unique prediction for a pressure-supported system,
  and in particular NO PREDICTED SIGN for the deviation of a dispersion-supported object from the modified-
  gravity kernel.  So:
    * 'Globular clusters sit below where dwarf spheroidals sit above' does refute the coarse reading f09 was
      chasing -- 'pressure support itself breaks the kernel', which is a SINGLE-SIGN hypothesis and is
      falsified by two signs.  That inference is sound and does not need modified inertia to have a sign.
    * It does NOT refute modified inertia, because an MI theory keyed to ORBIT SHAPE rather than to support
      type can accommodate both signs: globular-cluster stars are collisionally relaxed onto near-isotropic
      rosettes, dwarf-spheroidal stars are collisionless and may be radially anisotropic.  'Pressure-supported'
      is a coarser variable than the one Milgrom's theorem is about.
    * The claim as written says SUPPORT TYPE does not predict the sign.  That is what the data show.  Any
      stronger reading -- that the fork is closed, that modified inertia is excluded -- is not supported.
  [PASS] V10 g05 does NOT over-claim what modified inertia predicts: it nowhere states that the globular clusters exclude modified inertia, and its verdict block says in terms that the fork is untouched and still open.  The claim's second clause is about SUPPORT TYPE, which is the variable f09 actually used, and it is the right variable to falsify   (explicit fork disclaimer present = True; over-claiming phrases found = none)
  [FAIL] V11 (FAILS -- A FRAMING DEFECT, NOT A NUMERICAL ONE) S5's setup sentence asserts that under a reading in which pressure support breaks the kernel the clusters 'should behave like the dwarf spheroidals and sit ABOVE it'.  That assumes a SIGN for a hypothesis class that has none: no theorem fixes the sign of a modified-inertia deviation for a dispersion-supported system.  The falsification that actually goes through is the weaker and sufficient one -- a single-sign 'pressure support' hypothesis predicts ONE sign and the data show TWO -- and S5 should be stated that way rather than by attributing a prediction to the alternative   (the sentence is in the committed file; the numerical claim is unaffected, but the stated logic gives the alternative hypothesis a prediction it does not have)

======================================================================================================================
8.  HOW STRONG IS IT, IN ITS OWN CURRENCY?  The published statistics on the same data.
======================================================================================================================
  Gentile, Famaey, Angus & Kroupa 2010, A&A 509, A97 ran a Kolmogorov-Smirnov test on the SAME Pal 14 data
  against the SAME published MOND prediction and found only 64-73 per cent confidence (p = 0.27-0.36), i.e.
  NO exclusion.  The committed h93 records that gap and attributes it to the statistic, not the data.  The
  claim under test is correctly NOT quoted as a sigma; the check below is what the four residuals alone support.
  cluster      residual  +- from sigma_obs alone   N_RV
  Pal 4          -0.874                    0.180     23
  Pal 14         -1.306                    0.274     16
  Pal 3          -0.844                    0.369     22
  NGC 2419       -0.211                    0.082    183
  [PASS] V12 the four-cluster median is many times its own scatter-based standard error even before the measurement errors are folded in, so the claim is not an error-bar illusion -- but with N = 4 objects, two of whose dispersions are N-body model fits rather than measurements, and with a published KS analysis of the same Pal 14 data giving no exclusion at all, quoting this as a sigma would be wrong.  The claim does not, and must not   (mean -0.809 dex, scatter 0.451, standard error 0.225, ratio 3.6; per-cluster errors from sigma_obs alone are 0.08-0.37 dex)

